## Command chaining

- <!-- PowerShell --> Chain with `;`. **Never `&&`** — PowerShell rejects it as a token.
  For true short-circuit behaviour, check `$LASTEXITCODE` or use `if ($?) { ... }`.

## Heredocs and multi-line input

- <!-- PowerShell --> `$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)` is bash. PowerShell parses `<` as a reserved operator
  and the command dies before running. For a multi-line commit or PR body, write the text to a file
  and pass `git commit -F <file>` / `gh pr create --body-file <file>` (a path under `.git/` keeps it
  out of the working tree), or use repeated `-m` flags.

## Redirection

- <!-- PowerShell --> **Never `2>nul`.** There is no `nul` device; it creates a file literally named
  `nul` in the working directory. Use `-ErrorAction SilentlyContinue` on cmdlets or `2>$null` for native commands.
- Never redirect binary output with `>`. It re-encodes and corrupts the payload (this silently destroys
  screenshots pulled from a device). Write on the device/remote and pull the file instead.

## Search and file tools

- Prefer the `Grep` tool over `Glob` when either would work; `Glob` has returned zero results for
  patterns that exist.
- <!-- PowerShell --> For directory listings use `Get-ChildItem`, not `ls -R` or `find`.
- Use the dedicated read/edit tools rather than `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed`, or `awk`.

## Keep output small

- Filter noisy commands inline (`| Select-String -NotMatch 'warning:'`, `| Select-Object -First 20`,
  or `| grep -v` / `| head -20` on POSIX shells).
- Batch related git queries into one filtered call rather than three separate ones.
- Large tool output belongs in a throwaway subagent that returns a verdict, not in the main window.

## Line endings

<!-- Delete this section if the repo does not mix Windows checkouts with LF-normalised files. -->

- `core.autocrlf=true` locally while `.gitattributes` declares `* text=auto eol=lf`. Consequences:
  - `git status` lists files as modified that have **no content diff**. Confirm with
    `git --no-pager diff --stat` before believing a file changed.
  - `git diff` prints a wall of `warning: in the working copy of ...` lines. Filter them out.
  - **Never run the formatter repo-wide in write mode.** Scope it to changed files, with
    `--end-of-line auto` (or the formatter's equivalent).
  - A repo-wide format-check failure is not automatically "just line endings". Re-check the changed
    files scoped before dismissing it. CI on a clean runner is the authoritative check.

## Verify the artifact under test

- Before trusting any pass/fail seen on a device, emulator, container, or staging environment,
  confirm the running artifact is the one you just built (version string, hash, build timestamp).
  Hot-reload caches and stale installs have produced hours of phantom debugging.

## POSIX shells (bash / zsh)

<!-- If your machine uses bash/zsh, replace the PowerShell items above with these and delete the rest. -->

- `&&` and heredocs work; `2>/dev/null` is correct.
- Still never redirect binary output with `>` through a tool that may re-encode text.
- Still prefer `Grep`/`Read`/`Edit` tools over `grep`/`cat`/`sed` for file operations.
